                    coach_default:          `Move logged.`,
                    coach_r1_selective:     `<strong>Selective opposition is the strategic move.</strong> By endorsing the anti-teaser and anti-trap clauses — which match your brand — you earn the standing to contest the cap. You're a partner to the reform, not its enemy.`,
                    coach_r1_oppose:         `<strong>You're fighting all of it — including the clauses you support.</strong> You've just made yourself the enemy of the whole reform, and the Ministry's response is exactly the escalation it threatened: it bypasses the CRE and legislates harder. Which clauses should you <em>endorse</em> to earn the standing to contest the cap?`,
                    coach_r1_accept:        `<strong>Quiet acceptance protects the brand but forfeits €124M.</strong> You can move this rule — you don't have to absorb a cap below your hedging cost. Contesting the cap's <em>design</em> while backing the reform is not the same as lobbying against consumer protection.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp reading of the field.</strong> You triaged the clauses selectively AND located influence inside the consumer-protection logic the regulator already accepts. That is exactly the lens that lets you contest the cap without forfeiting legitimacy.`,
                    coach_r1_market:        `<strong>Arguing pure market efficiency cedes the field.</strong> The consumer associations own the public narrative here. Influence runs through the logic the audience already accepts — reframe inside consumer protection, not against it.`,
                    coach_r1_nologic:       `<strong>This is not pure interest-group power.</strong> A field governed by a consumer-protection logic punishes an actor who treats it as raw lobbying. Frame your ask inside the legitimate logic or you forfeit standing with the CRE.`,
                    coach_r1_kill:          `<strong>Demanding the whole cap be struck overreaches.</strong> 'Kill it' reads as self-interested and gives the regulator nothing to concede. Amending the cap to index it to hedging cost punishes teaser rates while sparing fair pricing — a credible ask.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>A position without numbers is just a complaint.</strong> The clause-by-clause EUR impact is what lets you argue the cap's <em>design</em> is wrong, not the reform. Quantify it rigorously or the regulator dismisses you.`,
                    coach_r2_shared:        `<strong>That reframing is institutional entrepreneurship.</strong> By anchoring your ask in consumer harm — a blunt cap hands the market back to the rip-off suppliers — you speak the field's logic, and the same request the commissioner would have rejected as self-serving becomes legitimate.`,
                    coach_r2_selfinterest:  `<strong>Your headline argument is your own margin.</strong> The commissioner reads it and thinks: 'so a supplier wants protection from a consumer-protection rule.' You've just confirmed the consumer association's narrative. How would the <em>same ask</em> sound framed around consumer harm?`,
                    coach_r2_technocratic:  `<strong>A dense economic note is necessary but not sufficient.</strong> Elasticity tables give the regulator cover, but with no public-interest narrative they don't move the politics. Pair the technocratic case with a legitimacy-bearing frame.`,
                    coach_r2_unbalanced:    `<strong>You've loaded one channel and starved the other.</strong> Inside and outside tactics are complementary: the submission and bilaterals land the ask, the public frame gives the regulator legitimate room to concede. Fund both.`,
                    coach_r2_overresearch:  `<strong>You've over-invested in commissioning research.</strong> Independent evidence lends credibility, but pouring most of a fixed €1.6M into one study starves the channels that actually deliver the ask.`,
                    coach_r2_overcounsel:   `<strong>Heavy reliance on external counsel reads as a hired gun.</strong> Past a point, a tiny in-house team outsourcing its whole campaign loses authenticity — and the cost crowds out the public frame.`,
                    coach_r2_noconcede:     `<strong>Conceding nothing publicly forfeits legitimacy.</strong> Backing the anti-teaser clauses you genuinely support is the price of the standing to contest the cap. Refuse to concede and you look like you oppose the whole reform.`,
                    coach_r3_lead:          `<strong>Leading the UFE shapes the position rather than inheriting it.</strong> You pay the cost, but you keep the SME disclosure relief your book needs in the industry's common position instead of losing it to the lowest common denominator.`,
                    coach_r3_join:          `<strong>Joining the consensus adds weight but cedes the agenda.</strong> You bind yourself to the common denominator. Was the position shaped to keep the SME relief you needed, or did you take what the big players wrote?`,
                    coach_r3_freeride:      `<strong>You stayed quiet and let the association carry you.</strong> But the UFE's consensus protects the incumbents and drops the SME disclosure relief that matters most to your book — €9M a year. You took the lowest common denominator because you didn't shape it, and the regulator reads industry silence as obstruction.`,
                    coach_r3_solo:          `<strong>Going it alone forfeits collective power.</strong> One non-incumbent's voice doesn't move the CRE. Without a coalition your credible ask has no weight behind it.`,
                    coach_r3_consumer:      `<strong>The consumer-group alliance is the masterstroke.</strong> On the anti-teaser clauses your interests genuinely overlap, so the ally is credible — and being seen alongside a consumer group on consumer protection is the strongest legitimacy signal you can send.`,
                    coach_r3_discount:      `<strong>You've joined the discount suppliers for raw firepower.</strong> But Le Monde runs 'Fair-price Hélios joins the rip-off suppliers to fight consumer protection.' Your €180M brand premium is the thing you just spent. Was the extra coalition weight worth converting a regulatory problem into a trust crisis?`,
                    coach_r3_incumbent:     `<strong>Accepting the incumbent's pact drops your SME relief.</strong> Their weight is real, but the pact sacrifices the disclosure relief worth €9M/year to your SME book — the very amendment you most needed. You bought power by giving up the ask.`,
                    coach_r4_both:          `<strong>Inside and outside reinforcing each other is the endgame done right.</strong> Your public frame gives the commissioner political room, and your private submission lands the specific amendment. That is the complementarity of non-market tactics.`,
                    coach_r4_insideonly:    `<strong>Quiet meetings with no public frame leave nowhere to land.</strong> The consumer associations own the narrative, and the commissioner can't be seen yielding to backroom pressure against a popular reform. What is your public frame doing for the private channel?`,
                    coach_r4_outsideonly:   `<strong>An all-out media campaign forces nothing and burns the brand.</strong> Attacking a popular reform in public corners the regulator into resisting you, and your fair-price reputation pays the bill. Outside tactics need a credible inside ask behind them.`,
                    coach_r4_public:        `<strong>The public-interest submission is the legitimate ask.</strong> Endorsing the reform and targeting only the cap's indiscriminate <em>design</em> gives the commissioner a public-interest reason to concede — exactly the room a margin argument would have closed.`,
                    coach_r4_margin:        `<strong>You led the submission with the €124M cost to Hélios.</strong> The commissioner reads a supplier asking to be exempted from consumer protection, and the journalist has the headline. The same amendment, framed around consumer harm, would have been defensible.`,
                    coach_r4_legal:         `<strong>A litigation threat antagonises the regulator.</strong> Signalling you'll sue if the cap stands hardens the CRE and reads as bullying a public-interest rule. It can win a clause and lose the relationship and the brand.`,
                    coach_r4_owns:          `<strong>Owning the fair-price record is the board-ready answer.</strong> Backing the anti-teaser ban while contesting the blunt cap shows the journalist you're a partner to consumers, not their enemy — that defends the €180M brand while you win the amendment.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>'No comment' cedes the story.</strong> Silence in front of a hostile journalist lets the consumer associations write your motives for you. Own your genuine fair-price record or the brand erodes by default.`,
                    coach_r4_attack:        `<strong>Attacking the consumer associations head-on torches the brand.</strong> You may move the clause, but 'Hélios goes to war on consumer protection' is a trust crisis. You protected the margin and spent the €180M premium.`,
                    coach_r4_norestraint:   `<strong>No restraint on the campaign risks a Pyrrhic win.</strong> You can soften the cap with an aggressive push and watch your Net-Trust score fall — €124M of margin protected, €180M of brand at risk. Hold back and defend the reputation while you win the ask.`,

